WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.990
We hope you’re ready, folks, because today
we’re discussing the most dangerous SCP

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.190
of all time.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:11.670
We know you’ve probably heard this before,
but seriously, this one is the absolute top

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.520
of the pyramid of fear.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.150
Or the Fear-amid, if you will.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:19.849
This SCP makes 682 weep in terror for its
life.

00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:25.669
It makes the Old Man, SCP-106, consider turning
his life around and taking up a career in

00:00:25.669 --> 00:00:27.289
humanitarian work.

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:32.669
It even makes the dreaded Scarlet King himself,
the Lord of Chaos, the Ultimate Despair, quake

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:34.760
in his spiky, red boots.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.980
It’s just that utterly terrifying.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.329
Just kidding, it’s Lego!

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:41.430
But we had you going there for a second, didn’t
we?

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:47.010
Though it may not actually be the scariest
anomaly of all time, SCP-387 isn’t your

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.350
grandad’s Lego set, either.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.079
Unless your grandad goes by Agent Hardy, and
works for the SCP Foundation.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:58.160
Hardy is one of the hundreds of thousands
of Foundation field agents planted all over

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.329
the globe, in everything from law enforcement
to surveillance, ever watchful for the kinds

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:09.040
of anomalies the SCP Foundation makes it their
mission to secure, contain, and protect.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.140
But when he discovered SCP-387, he wasn’t
even technically on duty.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.900
He was taking a long train journey home from
his parents’ house while his car was in

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.880
the shop, and as anyone who’s ever taken
a long train journey knows, without the proper

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.570
stimulation, boredom will crop up in no time.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:30.220
He’d been reading a pretty interesting book
on his e-reader, when tragedy first struck,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.950
and it ran out of battery.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.230
Hardy had forgotten to bring his charging
cable, so he was potentially looking at another

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.470
three hours of mind-numbing monotony.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:42.950
That’s when he noticed the red plastic tub
that had been apparently abandoned in the

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.460
seat across from him.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.350
He realized in that moment that it’d been
there the whole time, unsupervised.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:54.490
Given that he was a Foundation field agent,
and was, as such, extremely curious, he grabbed

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:59.140
the tub and took a look inside, hoping to
find something that’d quell his growing

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.140
boredom.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:05.500
Lucky for him, the box was full of Lego bricks,
everyone’s favorite brand of toy construction

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.500
block.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.520
The tub was brimming with a vast collection
of pieces, some of which - like wheels and

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:15.200
prisms - Agent Hardy had never seen before,
despite spending his childhood obsessively

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.200
making Lego constructions.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.780
But Hardy wasn’t one to criticize a gift
from the universe - this tub of Lego pieces

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.570
would be the perfect way to keep himself entertained
for the rest of his long journey.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:31.360
He spent the next hour or so meticulously
re-creating Site-19 in Lego form on the table

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.500
in front of him.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.870
When the building was done, he decided to
have some fun and populate the Site with a

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:41.630
collection of Legomen, standing in for SCP
Foundation scientists, containment specialists,

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:42.730
and guards.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.310
After finding a torso with a lab coat in the
box, Agent Hardy decided to make a tiny Dr.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.590
Bright to start with.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:53.050
However, as soon as he’d made the tiny Lego
scientist and set it down on his new Site-19

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:58.750
playset, he realized that everything was not
as innocently typical as it seemed : The little

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:04.360
man instantly began to walk, immediately picking
up a tiny Lego clipboard and heading towards

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.620
the Euclid containment wing.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.640
Agent Hardy had discovered a brand new anomaly
on his day off while playing with toys on

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.160
a boring train ride.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.680
Philosopher L.P. Jacks was certainly right
when he said, “A master in the art of living

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.640
draws no sharp distinction between his work
and his play.”

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.780
Naturally, Agent Hardy immediately took the
little Legoman apart, along with his Site-19

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:29.610
simulacrum, and alerted his superiors at the
SCP Foundation.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.610
His coworkers met him at the next train stop
platform.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:39.069
Not long after, the box of seemingly living
Lego pieces, redesignated SCP-387, was in

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:42.060
the hands of Foundation researcher Dr. Arch.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.380
His first priority was finding out what exactly
they were all dealing with here, whether there

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.880
was more of it out there, and whether it was
dangerous.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:54.370
Attempts to investigate the origins of SCP-387
were mostly fruitless.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.430
Undercover Foundation agents investigated
every Lego-producing location they could find,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.770
but found no evidence of anything similar
to 387 being produced.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:08.220
And of course, as with anything that seemed
to be an anomalous toy, all possible links

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.130
to Dr. Wondertainment were also explored.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.120
But again nothing came up.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.350
This was probably for the best - if 387 was
a Dr. Wondertainment product, it probably

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.919
would have built itself into an actual-size
dinosaur and started eating people already.

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:27.430
All further investigations into 387’s origins
were subsequently dropped.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:31.830
With background research turning out to be
a dead end, Dr. Arch decided to get down to

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.849
business doing the fun part: Actual experimentation.

00:04:35.849 --> 00:04:40.120
After gathering all the proper permissions
from his superiors, the testing commenced.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:46.389
In Experiment 387-a, Dr. Arch recruited a
number of Lego-loving D-Classes - after all,

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:51.479
even convicted robbers, arsonists, and murderers
love Lego - and made them construct a little

00:04:51.479 --> 00:04:54.449
Lego community inside a testing chamber.

00:04:54.449 --> 00:04:58.870
This consisted of a small main street with
a number of Lego stores, a residential area,

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:00.479
and a bar.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:05.129
As soon as the D-Classes constructed a variety
of little Legomen and released them into the

00:05:05.129 --> 00:05:10.199
community, they instantly came to life and
began performing their roles perfectly.

00:05:10.199 --> 00:05:15.509
Some took up jobs in the various stores, cafes,
and boutiques, Others acted as patrons for

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:17.080
the different businesses.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.319
The Legomen were even noted as forming rudimentary
family units.

00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:26.190
One man did fall into a river in this Lego
City, but otherwise, things were going smoothly.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:31.250
Encouraged by these developments, Dr. Arch
was ready to begin the true experiment: Introducing

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:36.629
a complex new variable into the environment
of the Legomen, and seeing exactly how they

00:05:36.629 --> 00:05:37.629
reacted.

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:41.870
He had another D-Class build a commercial
airliner out of normal, non-anomalous Lego

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:45.669
bricks, and place it just to the side of the
Lego community.

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:49.990
Over the following three hour period, Dr.
Arch observed the Legomen engaging in some

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.229
truly remarkable behavior.

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:56.009
Upon noticing the plane, the Legomen began
to form a plan.

00:05:56.009 --> 00:06:00.490
They first procured the necessary equipment
from the SCP-387 tub, and headed over to the

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:04.749
plane for a hard day’s work - in Legoman
time, at least.

00:06:04.749 --> 00:06:09.689
With remarkable efficiency, the citizens of
the Lego community began forming a fully functional

00:06:09.689 --> 00:06:11.309
airport around the plane.

00:06:11.309 --> 00:06:15.969
Multiple runways, an air traffic control tower,
and even a mall and food court within the

00:06:15.969 --> 00:06:17.539
airport itself.

00:06:17.539 --> 00:06:22.599
Tiny Lego attendants tried to sell perfume
to uninterested Lego commuters and Lego TSA

00:06:22.599 --> 00:06:25.939
agents frisked uncomfortable would-be Lego
passengers.

00:06:25.939 --> 00:06:30.349
Its accuracy to the real-life airport experience
was truly brilliant.

00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:31.819
But they didn’t stop there.

00:06:31.819 --> 00:06:36.819
A tiny Lego fuel truck sped over to the plane,
and acted out the process of refueling it.

00:06:36.819 --> 00:06:41.729
A Lego pilot and his Lego copilot climbed
into the plane, and it took off, literally

00:06:41.729 --> 00:06:45.770
flying around the room without any kind of
logical propulsion system.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:48.499
Dr. Arch was amazed.

00:06:48.499 --> 00:06:50.749
And even then, they didn’t stop.

00:06:50.749 --> 00:06:54.910
They continued to build more planes of various
makes and models, resulting in a sprawling

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:58.819
airport that, if you squinted, would look
just like the real thing.

00:06:58.819 --> 00:07:04.499
From this, Dr. Arch concluded that 387 Legomen
had the ability to convert non-anomalous Lego

00:07:04.499 --> 00:07:06.669
pieces into anomalous ones.

00:07:06.669 --> 00:07:09.090
He proceeded to the next experiment.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:14.689
For Experiment 387-b, Dr. Arch decided to
switch up the D-Classes and instead brought

00:07:14.689 --> 00:07:19.979
in some of Lego’s more traditional target
audience: A group of 10-year-old children.

00:07:19.979 --> 00:07:24.340
They began building some basic constructions
out of the pieces - your typical buildings,

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:28.939
people, and cars - but when they realized
that their creations were actually alive,

00:07:28.939 --> 00:07:31.110
their tune immediately changed.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:36.099
The kids spoke in hushed, excited tones about
a new plan, and then began putting it into

00:07:36.099 --> 00:07:37.099
practice.

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:41.309
They built functional Transformers - and just
to be clear, it wasn’t electrical transformers

00:07:41.309 --> 00:07:42.309
they crafted.

00:07:42.309 --> 00:07:48.210
We’re talking Transformers as in actual
Optimus Prime and Megatron, an M1 Abrams tank,

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.360
and a number of different Lego weapons of
mass destruction.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.499
Dr. Arch was mortified, realizing that he’d
apparently gathered up a team of budding childhood

00:07:56.499 --> 00:08:00.899
supervillains, and had them removed from the
test chamber for amnestic treatment immediately.

00:08:00.899 --> 00:08:06.520
However, the second the test chamber was vacated,
the kids’ creations came to life and entered

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:07.990
a state of all-out war.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:13.620
It was an intense battle, lasting about an
hour, and left no Lego survivors, just a huge

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:14.929
mess around the room.

00:08:14.929 --> 00:08:19.650
While this test was largely an embarrassment
for all involved, it did lead to some interesting

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:20.689
new data.

00:08:20.689 --> 00:08:24.650
Not only was this anomaly Safe in the sense
that it didn’t pose a containment breach,

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:29.569
it was also Safe in the more colloquial sense
- there was no way these Lego bricks could

00:08:29.569 --> 00:08:33.120
hurt you, unless you decided to eat them and
choked.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.699
Don’t do that, by the way.

00:08:36.699 --> 00:08:42.349
SCP-387 is unable to perform dangerous actions
if humans of any age are present in the vicinity.

00:08:42.349 --> 00:08:44.459
And this arrangement goes both ways, too.

00:08:44.459 --> 00:08:49.860
The Lego creations will be alive and, to some
extent, sentient, if played with peacefully.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:55.240
However, if the human playing with them becomes
hostile, then they will immediately become

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:56.290
inanimate.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:58.130
So play nice, folks.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:04.430
Next came Experiment 387-c and Experiment
387-c-2, where Dr. Arch turned his attention

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:05.970
to the tub itself.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:11.490
Since SCP-387 came into the Foundation’s
possession, the quantity of 387 blocks had

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:12.490
increased.

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:14.819
How is this possible, you’re probably wondering?

00:09:14.819 --> 00:09:20.380
Dr. Arch and his team discovered an interesting
phenomenon: When some of SCP-387 is placed

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:25.660
back in the red tub, but the red tub isn’t
filled, then 387 will self-duplicate to fill

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:29.930
out the tub - much like the way a gas will
disperse to fill out any containment unit

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:31.510
it finds itself inside.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:35.579
However, Dr. Arch also discovered some interesting
limitations.

00:09:35.579 --> 00:09:39.550
There seemed to be no inherent anomalous qualities
to the red tub itself.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:45.170
During one of the experiments, Dr. Arch half-filled
the red tub with non-anomalous Lego pieces,

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:46.990
and found that nothing changed.

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:52.350
Similarly, he performed another experiment
where he placed a single SCP-387 brick into

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:56.089
the box, and found there was no difference
when he came back.

00:09:56.089 --> 00:10:00.660
Dr. Arch and his associates came to the conclusion
that the anomalous properties are confined

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:05.269
solely to the bricks themselves, but they
have a kind of symbiotic relationship with

00:10:05.269 --> 00:10:09.690
the red tub, as they don’t replicate in
any other containment units.

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:14.339
In order to activate replication, the 387
must cover the entire bottom layer of the

00:10:14.339 --> 00:10:15.360
tub.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:21.100
Next came Experiment 387-d, where Dr. Arch
hoped to test the specific activation mechanisms

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:22.100
of SCP-387.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:27.840
The Lego creations animated whether they were
being assembled by a child or an adult, so

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.220
it wasn’t an age-regulated anomaly.

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:35.580
The next experiment involved using a remotely-controlled
robotic arm to create a Lego construction,

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:41.180
to see if the mere act of construction itself
was enough to instigate 387’s primary anomalous

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:42.180
effect.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.450
However, this didn’t turn out to be the
case.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:47.879
The robotic-arm-assembled Lego construction
never came alive.

00:10:47.879 --> 00:10:54.220
Dr. Arch’s next hypothesis was that perhaps
flesh was needed to activate SCP-387.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:58.600
But how could he test using flesh, without
just having a normal human assemble the Lego

00:10:58.600 --> 00:10:59.600
pieces?

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:02.720
Thankfully, the answer came to Dr. Arch by
total accident.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.509
A few cells over, an anomaly trying to breach
containment had just killed a Foundation agent

00:11:07.509 --> 00:11:09.260
trying to stand in its way.

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:14.569
While D-Classes cleaned up the mess, Dr. Arch
dragged the body over to where the 387 tests

00:11:14.569 --> 00:11:19.310
were being conducted, and meticulously puppeteered
it’s arms and hands to put together more

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.589
387 Lego constructions.

00:11:21.589 --> 00:11:24.839
But once again, it didn’t come to life.

00:11:24.839 --> 00:11:25.949
Interesting.

00:11:25.949 --> 00:11:29.100
Next hypothesis: Maybe it needed warmth to
activate?

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.680
In a move that proved unpopular with co-workers
just trying to eat their pre-packed sandwiches

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:38.509
in the Site-19 break room, Dr. Arch dragged
the dead Foundation agent’s body over toward

00:11:38.509 --> 00:11:43.220
the room’s radiator, and leaned its arms
and hands over the top of it for twenty minutes,

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:46.819
before giving his newly-discovered puppeteering
skills another go.

00:11:46.819 --> 00:11:48.470
But Once again, nothing happened.

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:53.190
Dr. Arch concluded that perhaps human psychic
energy, or maybe an active pulse, was what

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:55.360
caused 387 to activate.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:12:00.110
If you’re waiting for the horrifying twist
- well, aside from the puppeteering of a dead

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:04.399
co-worker’s limbs - we’re pleased to tell
you that there just isn’t one this time.

00:12:04.399 --> 00:12:09.860
SCP-387 serves no containment breach risk,
and no risk of harm to Foundation personnel.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:15.449
In fact, playing with the living Lego has
led to an 87% increase in morale for Foundation

00:12:15.449 --> 00:12:19.870
staff, so as long as you fill in the proper
forms and tidy up afterwards, you’re actually

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:23.920
allowed to play with the SCP-387 any time
you want.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:28.580
After all, working for the SCP Foundation
is a stressful job, and really, we’re all

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:29.610
children at heart.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:36.589
Oh, and there was one more experiment: Experiment
387-e: Where a Lego community was built using

00:12:36.589 --> 00:12:42.830
SCP-387, and then a pile of Mega Bloks - Lego’s
most common imitator - was introduced into

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:43.830
the mix.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:47.040
The Legomen of the community really weren’t
pleased by this.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.139
While the actual details of what happened
have thankfully been expunged, the one clue

00:12:51.139 --> 00:12:56.110
to what may have happened came from a note
left by Dr. Arch - that’s right, the puppeteer

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:57.110
guy.

00:12:57.110 --> 00:13:01.130
His notes on that experiment were four simple
words: “OH NO NOT AGAIN!”

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:08.069
Now go check out “SCP-1007 - Mr. Life and
Mr. Death” and “SCP-5094 - Miss J's Whiz

00:13:08.069 --> 00:13:11.959
Kidz Schoolhouse” for more fascinating safe-class
SCPs!

